A||||9||Banzai||Coffee can||||5||
Z||000000||Banzai||09-15-2001||08:32 PM||||started cleaning(blasting) the coffee can(vaccum resiviour) today and found a couple of holes in it. I'm surprised the engine ran IOK before the resto started. I'm assuming that the can is only in vehicles with cruise control. Seems like the holes would have caused vaccum leak symptoms.Anyone know where I can get a replacement? I thought the Fiero Store carried them but I can't find it online or in their catalog. <P>Thanks ||||reg||5
Z||000001||PBJ||09-15-2001||10:36 PM||||grocery store, first drain the apple juice out.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forsalestart.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||sprnt96||09-16-2001||12:02 AM||||A guy in our club makes custom aluminum ones..a little pricy but really sweet looking pcs||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||09-16-2001||12:17 AM||||You can replace the tin can with a plastic ball type. The vacball is available thru the MotorMite master catalog or you can get one from any salvage yard. <P>The vacballs with 2 ports have an internal check valve. Those can eliminate a separate valve that many Fiero use. The port you can blow air thru is the one that you connect to manifold vacuum. It's usually larger than the one going to the cruise or whatever...<P>Vacball is way better than the tin can. I don't think I've ever seen one of them go bad. Unless you are trying for some kind of factory resto for show use, use the plastic vacball.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Banzai||09-16-2001||10:06 AM||||Thanks I thought about trying to get an actual tin can but the valve assembly in the top of the can is hard plastic and could only be cut out. I'd like to know how they put it together.  Again thanks for the advise.<P>Banzai !!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||09-16-2001||03:24 PM||||any late model junkyard will be full of them, almost any car after 70's has a vacum tank somewhere under the hood.Offhand, 70's Lincolns I know have one thats very similar.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||09-16-2001||03:54 PM||||You can find the Vacballs in almost any car. GM, Ford, whatever. The ones with 2 ports have an internal check valve. Put manifold vac on the one port and everything else on the other. Does allot to clean up the vac line setup in allot of cars that have single port cans, external check valves, and T fittings...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Leadfoot||09-16-2001||09:35 PM||||I bought the ball from Napa for ~$9 bucks.  I used the original hold down bar off the metal can.  Try and place the plastic ball as far away from the exhaust pipes or it will deform from the heat.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||09-16-2001||11:50 PM||||Best place I can think of to mount vacball is over by the fuel vapor canister. Should be enough room there in any engine config. Most vacballs I've seen have mounting ears molded right on them. Depending on which motor, at worst you need to drill a hole or two. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif"> <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000009||MODIFIE||09-16-2001||11:50 PM||||use jb weld it works on almost any thing <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Banzai:<BR><B>started cleaning(blasting) the coffee can(vaccum resiviour) today and found a couple of holes in it. I'm surprised the engine ran IOK before the resto started. I'm assuming that the can is only in vehicles with cruise control. Seems like the holes would have caused vaccum leak symptoms.Anyone know where I can get a replacement? I thought the Fiero Store carried them but I can't find it online or in their catalog. <P>Thanks </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||12
